It was very hard. Might be in people who had private houses somewhere. But very few-- a couple-- very few people came. I heard people came from Slovakia, like, one or two woman, when they were deporting them. I think that was in '42. And maybe I heard about one or two from Poland. But I had no direct contact.

INT: Did the kehilla have any kind of contact with them?

EG: Probably-- not [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] openly, but probably under the table. You know, if somebody came, probably they helped them out some way, tried to help them. In Budapest, I know that they were more organized.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And they were helping the Polish refugees. Because there, not everybody knew everybody. But in a small town--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --was very difficult.

INT: Yeah. What about the synagogues? Did they--

EG: I don't know.

INT: [INAUDIBLE].

EG: I don't think so--

INT: Yeah--

EG: --because they were very scared and very afraid, and--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --it was more not the synagogues but the kehilla.

INT: Mhm. You said that your first real awareness was of the Warsaw ghetto uprising.

EG: Yes.

INT: How did you get that information?

EG: Radio London.

INT: Mhm. And what did--

EG: Oh, they were saying, you know, that blood is running and whatever it's happening. When we could-- first of all, it was very difficult even to get it--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in my neighbor's house, because they were making all kinds of noises. They didn't wanted the people to hear. And you were afraid that if somebody goes by, they going to hear it. But still, everybody listened, and they were very, very upset, you know? People were very, very upset.

But we didn't know anything like whatever happened. I, I mean, I never knew Belarus, where there Germans were going, you know, much before that. And all the massacres, we didn't know about.

INT: Mhm. What about the newspapers? Was anything reported in the newspaper?

EG: No. Not something like that, no.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There was garbage newspaper.

INT: When Hitler went into Poland, was that reported at all?

EG: Oh, sure.

INT: Yeah.

EG: That and-- there was the news and everything. Sure. He was winning, and all the whole blitzkrieg, sure.

INT: Mhm.

EG: It was like a euphoria. They were winning the war.

INT: And how did this impact on the Jewish community in--

EG: The Jewish community was very, very upset, and the Hungarian were very happy.

INT: That was my next question. How did that--

EG: They were very, very happy.

INT: --impact on you?

EG: The Hungarian were very happy.

INT: Did, did they in any way discuss this with you or treat you differently or--

EG: No. No, I didn't have. They didn't treat-- these are still Romanian sovereignty. When the Hungarian came in, in 1940, that's when they started, you know, treat you differently. Because they were-- like, classmates won't say hello in the street.

INT: Mhm.

EG: When you had the yellow star.

INT: Uh-huh When did things-- how did things begin to change when the Hungarians took over Satu--

EG: Oh, with ordinances. They came right away with ordinances. They came in, in the summer, and--

INT: What year?

EG: 1940. It was the dictum of Vienna. In Vienna, Hitler gave northern Transylvania and part of Czechoslovakia and part, part of-- [INAUDIBLE] to Hungarian-- to Hungary-- and northern Yugoslavia. And they came in, and the first rule, like, in the newspaper, they put out an announcement. September 5, school is going to start. And they ask the Jewish students not to show up for classes. Because there was something called numerus clausus.

Numerus clausus says 6% of the population is Jewish; 6% in every school can be Jewish. So they just picked one girl to be in the class, who was the bank director's daughter. And the bank director gave jobs, before, when the nun asked for poor Catholics. So they were in that [INAUDIBLE].

So that was one thing that-- and then-- so you couldn't go to school anymore, to the school that you used to go. Then they came out with the rule that if you have a store, you have to take in a Christian partner. You could not own alone. So that was called straw man-- it's straw men. So usually they picked one of the employees whom they trusted, and sometimes the employee stayed with the store.

And they made very difficult. They cut out the livelihood of the people. But they did not [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] endanger the life, per se. And then they brought in rules that the men between age of 20 and 40, they-- or 45-- they have to go to labor battalions.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They were, like, assisting the Hungarian Army that was advancing toward the Ukraine that time.

INT: Mhm. How long did they have to be in the army?

EG: There was no rule. They went and then they came back, and very many died--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --because in the Ukraine was very cold. And the situation was quite bad.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: And that time, till they were advancing, wasn't that bad. But when they had to retreat, and the situation started to be very bad.

INT: How did this affect you personally, as a family, you and your mother?

EG: We were afraid. We are in a steady state of fright, so to say.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We are afraid.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Us and everybody else, you know? People in the street, they spoke in whispers. And we knew something is not right.

But some people-- and some people, you know, economically they had very difficult times. But still, there was food, because the area is very rich in agriculture, and in the summer is very bountiful. And bread, and maybe they didn't have, you know, extra stuff, but bread and chicken and-- As a matter of fact, in the winter, goose, and they put it away. So food there was.

INT: Mhm. What happened to you for school after you couldn't go to the Catholic school?

EG: In Oradea Mare-- that was a neighboring city, about three hours by train-- that was a bigger city than Satu Mare-- that time, had [INAUDIBLE] [? 100,000 ?] inhabitants-- they had a Jewish school, four grades, gymnasium. And they had-- they made it bigger. They turned it into an eight-year school.

And the first year, I didn't go there, because we were five people and we hired a teacher, who was a professor, and we learned at home. And we went at the end of the year and we took the exams. And then after, I went there. And when I was in the eighth grade-- because you couldn't do it-- I mean, before baccalaureate-- was very hard, to school.

And I went, and I lived with a Jewish family. And was very hard, because sometimes I went home, and we were terribly afraid. And my mother didn't wanted to let me go, because they used to say that they throw Jews from the train. And it happened-- not with woman, but it happened. We heard that gendarmes or soldiers, you know, drunk, came and they saw a Jew, and they threw him out of the train.

INT: Mhm. How long did you go to the Jewish school in Oradea?

EG: In Oradea, I-- three years. Because I had five years' gymnasium from the Romanian-- during the Romanian-- and three more years.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I finished in 1944. That's the last class that had their baccalaureate--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in that school. That school is nonexistent today. And very few of the students are alive. There is an association. And when they met in Israel, and I couldn't go.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They came even from Australia and all over.

INT: Yeah. Yeah. Who supported you? How did you live in--

EG: We had some money.

INT: --in--

EG: Because my father had some money, and we had some-- a little money, not much money.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I mean, for-- you have to understand. [LAUGHS] I look today, you know, it's like poverty. That time was almost middle class.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because-- except, you know, the rich people?

INT: Mhm.

EG: But you could twist it around, and didn't spend much money. And in school I paid only half the tuition, because of my economic situation.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because we had to pay tuition--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --in school.

INT: Were there other rules for Jews at that time?

EG: No. Then after, you know, as years advanced, the ruling became stricter and stricter.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And when the Germans came in-- that was March 20 or 19--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --'44.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And right away--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the Jews couldn't come out from the house-- only certain hours--

INT: Yeah?

EG: --two hours in the morning, or two hours in the afternoon.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You had to put a yellow star of a certain size on your clothes. And what they did, when they came in, the first thing they did, they put together-- they took all the doctors, lawyers, Indian chiefs, you know, the rich people, so the Jews shouldn't have a leadership and shouldn't know what to do. And they beat them-- where is your gold? Where is your money? And we were under-- not exactly curfew but almost. You know, you--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --couldn't come out-- only to work. In the evening, never. You couldn't travel. You couldn't go no--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --place-- until-- but they made it very fast, because from March-- and in May we were already in ghetto.

INT: Yeah. Before 1944, while you were still in school-- the period from '41--

EG: Yeah.

INT: --to '44-- did anyone talk about leaving? Did people leave [INAUDIBLE]--

EG: There was no place. Where could you leave? There was absolutely no place where you could go. There was no way to go, nowhere. Because you are in Romania, where, where could you go? You couldn't go--

Some people who were smarter then, they crossed the border in Transylvania illegally, and ended up in southern Transylvania. And they, they got saved, because there they didn't deported the Jews--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in '44. Some people, in 1939, they went to Russia. Because when they took Bessarabia and Bukovina-- Czernowitz and Kishinev-- people-- some people with communist affiliation went there. Because there were, like, four or five days when the Romanians were leaving and the Russians still didn't come in. And those people, they had a terrible fate, because the Russians took them and they sent them in the back of the Urals, and many died. And they didn't have--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --a good reception.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because they were told, if you are real communists, you should have stayed and fought for the idea, and you shouldn't have left. And--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --there was basically-- because we are all surrounded already.

INT: Mhm. Did you have contact with your father during this time?

EG: My father, he some-- yes. You know, Jews always get the small [INAUDIBLE]. My father, if he had money, he gave it to a person, I don't whom, who wrote to Switzerland.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And then there were rich people, still-- Jews-- who probably had their money transferred to Switzerland. So sometimes a guy came-- no name, no nothing-- and deposited a sum of money-- like, $50, let's says, or $100 or something. Then that was the way, you know? That here, somebody [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] knew, you know-- from here you could transfer, I guess, money to Switzerland,

INT: Mhm.

EG: --that time. And then those people, who charged probably a certain percentage, found a rich Jew who they said, look, you have so much money. What's it going to be? You put it in Switzerland.

INT: Mhm. Did you have any correspondence with your father-- not just money--

EG: No, no. No, you couldn't write anymore--

INT: You had no-- when did correspondence stop? When did relationship stop?

EG: Basically, I think, in '39, when the war--

INT: When the war broke out. Mhm. Yeah.

EG: Very little. My father has-- had two sisters who lived in Amsterdam-- in Antwerp, in Belgium. And my mother used to correspond with them, and it stopped when the Germans were there. And once we got a, a postcard [CRYING] from them. And it said that we are OK, but Uncle [NON-ENGLISH] is a very [CRYING] rare visitor.

INT: Mhm. Meaning bread. Mhm.

EG: That they don't have bread.

INT: Mhm. Yeah. Mhm. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] What happened to you personally when you came back from Oradea, had graduated, and the Germans came in?

EG: No, I graduated in June, and the German came in in March. I was teaching. I was--

INT: They came in after you came back.

EG: Yes.

INT: Yeah.

EG: Yes. In-- it was, like, an eight-month difference. I was teaching poor students. As a matter of fact, one of them was in the convent. It was a Catholic girl.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I was giving classes.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I was making some money.

INT: Mhm. And your personal life, what do you recall of that period?

EG: Personal life--

INT: First couple of months.

EG: --well, we were going with my friends, and that was it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But it wasn't a big life, because-- you know, there were rich girls who used to go into Budapest. They still could go in '43. And they were in Budapest and very elegant and-- from my class.

But we were together. We were friends. We are visiting each other. And I was teaching.

And we are thinking, you know, this and that. The men had, you know, more Zionistic organizations. The woman had very little.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But by that time, there was no organization, because they didn't allow. Here and there came a certificate from--

INT: Mhm.

EG: the Palestine or the English, and some people left, but very few.

INT: Mhm. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] When was your, your, your first personal contact with Germans?

EG: My first personal contact with Germans? When I arrived in Auschwitz. I mean, I saw them in, in Satu Mare. The whole thing was done by the Hungarian, the deportation and putting you in wagons, and all the story was done by the Hungarian. I don't think there were 10 Germans in the whole city.

INT: Yeah.

EG: That was all done by the Hungarian, mainly by the gendarmerie.

INT: Mhm. You said, earlier, that they, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they mistreated and separated the wealthy or the intellectuals--

EG: Yes.

INT: --from the--

EG: Yes, in the--

INT: Who--

EG: But they let them out--

INT: --who were leaders? Yeah.

EG: They let them out in the ghetto. They put them in the ghetto.

INT: Mhm. So what was the leadership? Who were the leaders? Who could people turn to?

EG: In the ghetto, they had-- how should say-- Judenrat or somebody.

INT: Yeah. Who was Judenrat?

EG: It was a man, Mr. Schwartz was his name. He was a very nice man, but he couldn't do nothing.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I mean, there were-- there was some kind of organization. Like, they brought in, in the beginning, food. And they had, for people who came from the villages who couldn't bring enough food, they had a kitchen, a soup kitchen. And they were giving out-- it was very short.

INT: Yeah.

EG: The whole thing was very short.

INT: Yeah.

EG: So there was food. And the apartments that they had a designated area of the city. And they pushed in as many people as they could in each room.

INT: OK. How were you told to leave your house?

EG: By word of mouth, we were told-- oh, you mean, to go to ghetto, or to go to--

INT: To go to ghetto.

EG: Oh, to go to ghetto? A carriage came, and some soldiers came, and the [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] gendarme came. And they came, and they said, take a suitcase, and go. And they threw the suitcase on the carriage, and we walked after.

INT: How much time did they give you?

EG: Nothing. We knew already that they're going to come.

INT: You knew.

EG: And they took the neighbor.

INT: Did you plan for it at all, you and your mother?

EG: Yeah, we had in a suitcase packed up.

INT: Yeah. What did you take with you?

EG: We took some clothes and some-- as a matter of fact, we took my mother got, because they told, you know, that-- I don't know where we go-- even we got a piece of bacon, a big piece of bacon. I don't know-- he gave a coat, because he-- you couldn't take it-- to a peasant and gave him a big piece of bacon. But we never touch-- but they said, you know, that-- to have what to eat. And that's the only thing that, in the summer, lasts. And food. We packed up--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --some food and some clothes.

INT: Mhm. What happened to jewelry, furniture, any valuables?

EG: They were taking all the valuables before. They were going and taking away. And people hid or gave it to somebody. We gave-- my mother had a gold watch, and I had a couple pieces that I gave to one of my students.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And she hid it-- and some linen. Because, you know, for girls you used to make already. So I have-- even, I have it, today, all embroidered, tablecloths and--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --a couple-- I never used them. But [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they were just looking. And they took out-- if you had any little earring, wedding band, they took away everything--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --silver candlesticks--

INT: Did you hide anything?

EG: Yes.

INT: Where did you hide?

EG: One of my students-- I gave her--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --some little jewelry. I had a gold bracelet that I got when I was 16. I had a pair of earrings. I have a little medallion that I still have them. And my mother had a gold watch, a gold [NON-ENGLISH] [? watch. ?]

She, she was well-meaning. So she hid everything in the earth. And when we came back, she gave it back to me. so the watch, you could throw it away. Because the watch, if you dug it in the earth--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --is not going to [LAUGHS] work anymore.

INT: Yeah. You, you hid things in the earth.

EG: Yeah-- she, she did.

INT: Uh-huh. Did anybody know where things were hidden?

EG: No.

INT: No. When you left--

EG: I gave to this woman.

INT: When you left, who watched you leave?

EG: What?

INT: When you left-- you left your house--

EG: Yeah.

INT: --how did you go?

EG: We just walked out. [INAUDIBLE].

INT: And was anybody watching you walk?

EG: The policemen and the--

INT: Neighbors?

EG: --gendarme. Neighbors? Probably. I don't know.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They knew it [INAUDIBLE].

INT: Anybody say anything?

EG: No. No, nobody said nothing.

INT: Mhm. How far was the ghetto from where you were living?

EG: Very near-- a couple blocks.

INT: Mhm. Did the-- what-- did the ghetto have a wall or fencing or anything like that?

EG: It had, like, a door. They picked, you know, a street.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And where the street-- from one side of the other side of the street, they put up a fence.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But-- and they had somebody watching it. But basically, you could get out if you wanted very badly. But there was no place where to go.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There was nobody to hide you.

INT: Yeah.

EG: Unless there were a couple people who got papers, and they went to Budapest right away. You couldn't stay in the town, because they recognize you. As a matter of fact, there were, like, a couple families who had-- the father was in the First World War and got the silver cross. That's about the biggest decoration you can get.

And if you had a silver cross, you were exempted from the Jewish laws. And those people, they couldn't stay-- they were not deported. And after a couple of days, they had to go to Budapest.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because they were going in the street, and they were telling, they took all the Jews away. What are you doing here?

INT: Mhm.

EG: So they got scared, and they went up-- because in the capital, they haven't been known. But [INAUDIBLE] three families, I think.

INT: Yeah. Do you recall where you stayed in the ghetto?

EG: Yes. In the ghetto, I stayed with a family. They told you, you go in this street, and I went into a room with my mother. And there was somebody else there also, another, I think, two or three people.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we stayed very little, because if we arrived in the ghetto early May, they already started, by the 20th of May, to take away people--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --from the ghetto. But because I was working in the ghetto office-- real estate, so to say, where, you know, they were putting people in the apartments, so I didn't have to go. I didn't have to go with the first transport. Because they said, this street goes first. This street goes second.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And when we got to the third transport, I told my mother that, look, I think it's better if we leave, even though I don't have to leave, because who knows where we are going. And if we get first, maybe we'll a better accommodation or a better job.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because we are all told that we are going to go to a place-- they called it Kenyérmezo-- a nonexistent Hungarian town-- and we are going to work.

INT: Say that again?

EG: Kenyérmezo. That's nonexistent.

INT: Oh.

EG: 'Kenyér' means bread. And 'meso' means-- 'meso' means 'land.'

INT: Mhm.

EG: So-- but, you know, the stories were that we are going to go, and we are going to work, because the Germans need our work. Nobody expected this butcher ride that came.

INT: Did you know anything about concentration camps?

EG: No. We heard the word Dachau, maybe, that homosexuals were there and Germans were there, you know--

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: --but nothing else. The word Auschwitz? Never in the dreams-- or none--

INT: No.

EG: --of these concentration camps.

INT: OK. You said you worked in the ghetto. What kind of work did--

EG: I--

INT: --what was real-estate [LAUGHS] work?

EG: Real-estate work was that they gave you a piece of paper, and they told you, go in this street and measure every room, and write it up. [INAUDIBLE] and they give a certain amount of square footage per a person. So the average room should have five, six people in it. But that lasted very short, because when they started emptying the streets, people could move into the empty apartments--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --for the very short time.

INT: Mhm. Were you paid for your work?

EG: No. Nobody was paying. Nobody expected to be paid.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The idea of working was that if you work, you're going to be treated better or saved or I don't know what organization--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --should I say. But I left Satu Mare with the third transport. And the very sick people and the people with power pushed everybody else to go. So the fifth transport, when got to Auschwitz-Birkenau, we all went to Lager C. Lager C. They were all killed--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --almost-- very few people from the fifth transport.

INT: Mhm.

EG: As a matter of fact, [INAUDIBLE] I had three classmates. One, the father was a director of the bank. The other, the father was a very wealthy merchant. And the third one, they were big land owner. As a matter of fact, they had even a [? 'von' ?] in front of the name.

And when they got their baccalaureate-- I couldn't go to college, because there was no college.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And they wouldn't admit it. But there was a very fine finishing school in Budapest. And they went there. It's called the English Ladies. And there, you learn languages, play piano, serve-- you know?

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: And they went there. And they were in Budapest. And in Budapest, many people were saved.

But here, the fathers got together, and they were very smart people, and they hired a retired army colonel from the Hungarian Army, whom they paid as much money, like, to buy a house. And he went up to Budapest and took the three girls back home. So be smart. If they had left them there, they would be alive.

INT: We're going to change our tape now.INT: Tape number three, interview with Eva Green. In the ghetto, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] other than working, what, what did people do all day? What did you do all day?

EG: People didn't work on the ghetto. There was no work.

INT: But you worked.

EG: I work-- that took two hours or three hours a day. It wasn't work. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] maybe people-- some woman worked in the kitchen. Because they had a very big soup kitchen, what gave food to people who didn't have food. They didn't do nothing.

INT: What'd they talk about?

EG: They talked between themself, and they cried, and they were upset, and they were scared. And they had children to tend. And the-- it was so short that no organization came out from this, you know? There--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --was nothing to do. Sometimes in the evening, some people who had-- from the Christian people, they went to the gates, and some people, they spoke a couple words. That was about--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --all the contact with the Christian people. But there was no effort from nobody to save one single Jew.

INT: But amongst yourselves, your friends--

EG: Yeah.

INT: --did you talk about anything, like did you--

EG: We didn't know. We were puzzled. We didn't know. We didn't know what's happening.

INT: Who-- was there any spiritual advice? Like, what did the rabbis do, or the leaders, political leaders--

EG: The rabbi, nothing.

INT: Did people daven?

EG: The very religious ones, the Hasidim, davened. The rest of them, not that much.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You know, just to tell you how the leadership didn't know anything. When I was measured into an apartment, I went-- there was a man who owned a bank. And it's an old local family. They were like the creme de la creme. And all of a sudden, a goyishe governess shows up with two little children. They were the children of their daughter who lived in Sighet.

And she felt that if she sends her two daughters to her father, who was the omnipotent man in Satmar, the children going to be saved. So the goyishe governess-- then the goyishe governess went out of the ghetto, she-- and I, I was there, present, when the two little girls arrived.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because they thought that this man-- this man--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --was as powerless as the--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --most powerless--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --poor Jew.

INT: You were there for what, three weeks in the ghetto, four weeks?

EG: Something like that, yeah.

INT: Yeah. In daily life, did-- what happened if someone got sick?

EG: There were doctors there. There were. There very many doctors, even though, when the Germans came in, the first day, I think 11 doctors killed themself in Satmar.

INT: Killed themselves?

EG: Yes.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They committed suicide.

INT: Oh. And did anyone die there?

EG: Yes.

INT: How did you bury?

EG: They-- I think they let out, to go, a couple people, to the Jewish cemetery. If you died, they let you go in this. But people who committed suicide. There were suicides.

INT: Were babies born there too?

EG: Yes.

INT: Oh. Anybody get married or have a bar mitzvah?

EG: No, I don't think so.

INT: There-- OK.

EG: Bar mitzvah, maybe in the shul they gave him an aliyah.

INT: Mhm, yeah

EG: But-- no.

INT: Mhm. Were you ever searched in the ghetto?

EG: No.

INT: Did any of the girls get searched?

EG: Not to my knowledge.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I don't think so. Maybe there was one or two people whom they thought that has, you know, very rich and gold hidden, but I--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --don't think so.

INT: Mhm. What do you remember most? What was a thing that you remember most from the ghetto experience?

EG: Oh, when I realized that this is something very bad. I mean, I wasn't very old. I was only 18.

There was a family-- also wealthy people-- who paid somebody to take them over through Romania. And half of the family went through, and the other half were caught. So they were brought back. And when I was there in the ghetto office, that's when they brought them back.

And I asked them, did they do anything to you? They said no. I figured, if there is nothing being done to them, and no punishment and no nothing, that means that this is the worst place. They just dumped them in the ghetto.

And my mother had a cousin who lived in a small village with her husband. They had-- were farmers. And they had a little boy, maybe 13.

And this man, [? Pinchas, ?] he wasn't a poor man, but he had boots, you know, and the [INAUDIBLE], like farmers. And his village was a village full of Romanian, very few Hungarian. He made, at the back of his other bunker-- and what he had-- flour and fat and everything-- he gave to this peasant whom he trusted. And he hid with his wife and his child.

And every evening, the peasant came and gave him food. And he always asked him, what's happening? What's happening? What's happening? Said, the Germans took people from this village, people from that village. And then one day said, there are no more Jews here. They took all the Jews from the villages.

And he got so scared. He said, what's going to happen to him? And he got himself out from the bunker, with his wife and his kid, and they showed up in the ghetto in Satmar, voluntarily.

INT: Mm.

EG: And the Russians, already in October-- this was in [INAUDIBLE] October-- they were there. Because people didn't know. He, he was very afraid. He was figuring there is no more Jews, no more Jews. In Satmar, they are taking away the Jews from the ghetto in Satmar. What's going to happen to him?

INT: Mhm.

EG: You know, without contact, without knowledge. He had a idea in the beginning, but he couldn't go through. He got very scared.

INT: Yeah. How did you leave the ghetto?

EG: We could take a very small package, and we had to go in line. They typed our names. And they took us to the train station.

INT: Mhm. You offered to go?

EG: Yes. I went voluntarily.

INT: How? Who did you go to?

EG: I, I showed up.

INT: You just went--

EG: I showed up and I said my name.

INT: Your mother too?

EG: Me and my mother. Because they had a quota. And they couldn't care less. They-- a quota was 3,000.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So there was no difference if Green goes or Klein goes, as long as it was 3,000. The last one, probably, they were more thorough, because they didn't wanted to leave nobody there.

INT: Mhm. Where, where did you go? Where did you show up?

EG: At the-- they had, like, a long table, and three or four typewriters, and three or four woman--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --typing the names. And you go there, and they take you.

INT: And, and then where did they take you?

EG: We walked to the train station.

INT: And how far was that?

EG: You know, it's a small town. I don't know, 15, 20 blocks, something like that. We took our packages.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We took some food. And they allowed, I think, a, a backpack or a small suitcase.

INT: Mhm. And then what happened?

EG: Then we got there. They put, like, 70, 80 people in each cattle car. But somehow, we were, like, the last ones, so we were in the luggage compartment, so to say, because they took all-- with all the luggages--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --away, because, you know, it was a grabbing-- everybody, the police and the gendarmerie, whatever they could grab from the Jews, they were really ready. So this was full of, of suitcases, and we are like 15 people there. So it was more human in that sense. There was not more room, but we had only about 12 people or 15 people.

INT: Mhm. Were you with your mother?

EG: Yes, with my mother.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: And there was a very religious family, and the man was davening. And at the end, they were screaming that no gold, no silver. And he had a tallis. And they took off the silver, you know--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --how just--

INT: [INAUDIBLE].

EG: And they threw it through the window during the night.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because if they catch you, you never knew-- we didn't know where we are going. But there was a window, and through the grates-- the first time when I really got scared was when I looked-- I don't know-- after a day or two-- [NON-ENGLISH]. And that was Poland. So then we knew that it's the end.

INT: You knew you were going to Poland.

EG: Poland.

INT: Why do you say you knew that this is the end? What--

EG: Because we knew that Polish Jews and-- in Poland, people were treated terribly. Like, even in the '40s, you had to prove that you are Hungarian or Hungarian citizen or born there. And people were in Poland-- from Poland, because many Polish Jews came during the First World War-- because it was-- the whole thing was Austro-Hungary. And those people were put back through the frontier. And we knew that they--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --were all killed there.

INT: Mhm. Did you have food on the train?

EG: Yes, we took some food with us, yes.

INT: Mhm. How long were you on the train?

EG: About four days, I think. What we had very little was water.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We had to ask, and sometimes they gave us, the soldiers who were there.

INT: Mhm. Had you seen any Germans at that point?

EG: Yes, already.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The trains were with Germans.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Very few-- I mean, they wouldn't spend their German Army.

INT: Yeah. Anyone get sick on the train?

EG: With us, no.

INT: No.

EG: No, we were not that many.

INT: When did you get to Auschwitz?

EG: But very many people had-- people died. Not in my--

INT: Yeah. No, I meant in your car.

EG: In my car, no, because we are not that many.

INT: Mhm.

EG: In Auschwitz-- I think it was May the 25th or 26th, something like that.

INT: Yeah. What time of day did you get there?

EG: I think it was early afternoon. And--

INT: Can you recall or picture it?

EG: Yeah. We got down from the train. And we had to, to take, you know, the little suitcase. And the prisoners came, all Polish men, dressed in the striped uniform.

And I was going with my mother, so he said in Yiddish that is-- who is she? So I said, my mother. She said, no. She's your sister. And she's 29. And then he went away. Because he figured--

And then Mengele came. And you know-- right, left, right, left. Don't think that it took him a second to think over that this person is going to go to the right, or this child is going to go to the left.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Chut, chut, chut, chut, chut, chut. We-- we haven't got the slightest idea. We saw that-- I don't know-- en masse, if we had [INAUDIBLE], we would have felt more familiar.

INT: Mhm.

EG: First, we didn't know, who are these people? Who are these people in uniform? And, you know, the chimney and the red flames-- it was a very terrifying experience. And dogs barking. That was the German style. You know, the German officers walked with those dogs.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And like Mengele, dressed up like, you know, going for a reception.

INT: Did you know he-- who he was?

EG: After, in the camp, I learned--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --that his name was Dr. Mengele--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and he was--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --the camp doctor. But when I got down, I never heard his name.

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: I didn't know anything about him.

INT: Uh-huh. And what happened-- what was the process, the procedure, after--

EG: The procedure? We were together, and we were taken into Lager C-- Lager C in Birkenau.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And they had these wooden-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they were not bunks-- it's bunk. So [INAUDIBLE] we were told, six in one side and six in the other side. Six in one side, six in the other side.

So you just could lay down, half side, and the other person had to put his foot where your head was, you know?

INT: Mm.

EG: Like sardines.

INT: Yeah.

EG: And Our kapo came. What was-- not kapo, because she was the blockalteste. That was--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the elder of the block. She was a very religious woman from Slovakia. Her father was the rabbi from [NON-ENGLISH].

INT: She was Jewish.

EG: Sure. She was the daughter of the rabbi from [NON-ENGLISH]. She comes from the [NON-ENGLISH] family.

INT: Oh, mhm.

EG: I think her name even was [NON-ENGLISH]. I don't know [INAUDIBLE].

INT: Mhm.

EG: But she was there already two and a half years, and you know, she got tough. She wanted to live. She knew what was there before.

INT: In what way was she tough?

EG: She was tough, like, people asked, you know, crying, where is my mother? And she had, you know, that walking stick. She said, there, in smoke. So where is my child, you know? Young mother-- up in smoke. Things like this, you know? She didn't have to baby anybody, but--

INT: Mm. How many were you in that C lager?

EG: I think they had 31 barracks. Each barrack had approximately 1,000 people.

INT: Mhm. Did you know anyone there?

EG: Yes, from my hometown, and from people-- yes--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --sure, I knew--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --people there. And we had one latrine, going to the end. And once we went to the latrines, and we saw a lot of men come in. They were cleaning. They were-- and I recognized doctors from my hometown. And, you know, one or two words, you couldn't talk.

So they, they asked, who is a doctor, the Germans. And I don't know how many raised their hands, you know, [INAUDIBLE] in so many Jews, you find plenty doctors. And they told them, no, you go and you clean the latrine.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So it was very-- early in the morning, you had to get up and go in lines of five. And it took three, four hours, at least, to count the people. They never ended up-- I don't know why.

They came, the Germans, with the dogs. And sometimes-- you know, they had walking sticks, and they were just hitting, for the pleasure of hitting.

INT: Were-- did you still have your own clothing in that C Lager?

EG: No.

INT: Oh.

EG: No, no, no, they took away everything.

INT: How did that process work for

EG: You had to go to the bath.

INT: When? The first day, or--

EG: After the arrival, when--

INT: After-- uh-huh.

EG: You went to the bath. They shaved your hair. And they gave you some rags, so to say.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Only did the shoes, they left.

INT: Mhm. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Was your mother with you the whole time?

EG: Yes.

INT: The kapo, or the blockalteste, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] did some people have a better time with her than others? Did she have favorites?

EG: She-- her sister ran away from Slovakia and was in Hungary, in Csenger, and she took her right away. And she was supposedly engaged to somebody, and that man's sister came. And right away-- she was very religious. Right away, she got her a kerchief, to cover her hair, because it was [INAUDIBLE] this woman.

And they were with her in the-- they had, you know, like, a special bunk--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in the front.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I don't know if she-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] her-- I don't want to excuse her, but she tried, you know, to comply with what the Germans wanted, because I guess she was afraid for herself.

INT: Mhm.

EG: She had the experience. She was there for two and a half years. If you survive two and a half years in Auschwitz, you know--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --your outlook of life--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --it's a little bit changed.

INT: Yeah. Were you sick at all?

EG: No.

INT: Mhm. Your mother?

EG: No.

INT: Mm.

EG: I was sick later in Hainichen.

INT: Mhm. Mhm. Were you aware of the time, the dates, the Jewish holidays, Shabbos?

EG: Yes.

INT: Did anyone have a calendar?

EG: Yes. I don't know, calendar. I don't know how, but people knew it. As a matter of fact, when Yom Kippur came, nobody-- [CRYING]

INT: Yeah.

EG: --maybe they ate a little bit later, before-- or a little bit [INAUDIBLE] after. But Yom Kippur was-- the majority--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --of the people kept it. And we knew Rosh Ha-Shana, and we knew Sukkot.

INT: Mhm. How?

EG: I don't know. But people said, Sukkot is this, is that.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They knew-- probably, there were women who knew-- you know, were very familiar with the Hebrew calendar--

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: --between so many people. [INAUDIBLE] herself, I am sure that she knew. Or maybe she had a--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --some kind of calendar or something.

INT: Yeah.

EG: Because she was already the blockalteste.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But her situation also was very precarious--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --because today you are blockalteste, and tomorrow they put you in smoke.

INT: Yeah. Did you have a number at all?

EG: No.

INT: No.

EG: No because, in Lager C-- they called it vernichtungslager, so annihilation lager. So nobody had.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The next lager to us [INAUDIBLE] they all had.

INT: How did they count you then, on-- at the zahlappell?

EG: We stand in lines of five, and they had to count. They had a list-- I don't know-- that this block has 900 people or 950--

INT: But did you have a name there?

EG: No, no, nobody had a name.

INT: No. No. Just-- mhm.

EG: Nobody had a name.

INT: Mhm. Did you work?

EG: No. Nobody worked. Here and there, they took a person, but--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --nobody worked.

INT: Yeah. So what, what did you talk about to, to the other inmates?

EG: In the beginning, well, things that were home or after. And when the hunger started, I don't know nobody put the emphasis on this, and it's a proven fact. Women giving each other recipes, food. And the richer and the fatter the food, [LAUGHS] the better the recipe.

INT: Mhm.

EG: For the nonexistent [LAUGHS] food.

INT: Yeah.

EG: Because the food was terrible, in the beginning. The first three days, I couldn't eat. I had to push-- they gave us a soup-- they called it [NON-ENGLISH]-- that had all kinds of animal feed, like [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] turnips, but not the regular turnips. You know, what you give to the cows. And it wasn't washed, or it wasn't peeled.

That pieces of turnip and some kind of other vegetable. If you could get a peel of a potato, that was like the most--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --delicacy that you could imagine. In the morning, we got black coffee, nothing else. Then for lunch, they gave this terrible soup. And in the evening, they gave some bread that had even sawdust. And supposedly it had bromide to calm people. And some pills they said that people should stop having menstruation.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And they had, like-- I don't know how much you have, been half a pound, I think,

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and a piece of some kind of cold cut. That was also very little, like, pork cheese or some-- pork cheese, I think, was in, in most of the cases.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: Very small and very slim. And from the bread, you are supposed to save for the morning coffee, because for the morning coffee they didn't give you nothing.

INT: Mhm. How did people get along?

EG: You know, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] exceedingly well. It's unbelievable, but there was no violence. Sometimes people, you know, had a fight or words.

But there was no violence, as far as I know. It was very, very little violence. I don't think that-- between any other type of people than the Jewish people, all this huge amount of people, and there was no violence.

INT: Mhm.

EG: No violence. Sometimes was something, that you did this, you did that, or this is my place, this is your place. Sometimes people said that they saved a piece of bread. They put under the head, and it disappeared. But very minor things. Basically, was no-- not much.

INT: What'd you do for sanitation? How did you keep clean?

EG: You went and you washed yourself in cold water.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They had faucets, that you could go-- if they let you. Once a day, once every other day, or once every three days.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because they-- it was very difficult. Like, for the toilet use, you could go only when it was allowed. And when they had something going on, like actions, like they were taking away people, then they had lagersperre. That means they closed the lager. And you could wash, but you couldn't go.

INT: Mhm.

EG: When they had, you know, lagersperre-- screaming, and the dogs barking-- so you couldn't go.

INT: Mhm. What was the hardest part for you, that, that particular period?

EG: I mean, being there. That was the hardest part.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You didn't know what's happening. By that time, after a couple days, we were very well aware of the crematoria. But people had a tendency-- some people, you know, from the psychological disposition-- that they said they took them here, and maybe they took them there, and maybe they are alive. They didn't face reality.

But we knew reality, deep down inside. We knew reality, and we knew that [INAUDIBLE] you have very small chances getting out, except from the smoke.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Some people, a couple times, were taken to work. But we--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --haven't been taken.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And once we were taken to the baths.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we took a hot bath there. But that was also very traumatic, because if you went there, you always had it in the back of your mind that the bath might be not for washing but for killing purpose.

INT: Mhm. Were you aware of that?

EG: Yeah, sure.

INT: Of the showers?

EG: Yes.

INT: How did you become aware of that?

EG: We heard it from the people.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I mean, you know, you know that-- we knew that there was a Sonderkommando. You know, sometimes people came, and that, every six weeks, you know, those people were killed--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --because they didn't wanted witnesses or whatever--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the reason was.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But we didn't see-- we saw very little men. We, we didn't see-- only the German who came in.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And--

INT: Was there any relationship at all with men and women-- between men and women?

EG: Not in this lager, no.

INT: Mm. Mhm.

EG: A German old man who-- they were mainly Volksdeutsche. They were very primitive people--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --these German guards.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Don't think that they were the creme--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --de la creme. There were very primitive people, mainly-- some of them were from Transylvania-- Volksdeutsche, you know--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --who were offered the job. And they were--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --like--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --very low echelon of the society in camp. And they enjoyed themself. They could be as sadistic as they want.

INT: Was there a relationship at all between the Jewish prisoners and-- men and women? Did you have any contact with them at all?

EG: Very, very--

INT: Mhm, yeah.

EG: --rarely, very rarely. There was some, but--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --very rarely.

INT: Yeah. Any-- was there any kind of relationship between women and women in the--

EG: Not to my knowledge.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I heard somebody saying, but in my lager-- you know, some-- the sexual part-- people were so desperate and so hungry and so tired that I, I think, if there was, there was very little, whoever said. Maybe there was, here and there, a girl who [INAUDIBLE]-- you know, was picked up by a German, maybe. I have no knowledge about it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I would say, I didn't hear it.

INT: Mhm. How long were you in, in Auschwitz?

EG: Till October.

INT: Uh-huh. And then what happened?

EG: Then we had a very big selection.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And my mother and I were selected to go in--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --to Germany to work.

INT: Mhm.

EG: That time was already-- I, I think that day was the crematoria-- they ended-- or the day before, you know?

INT: Mhm.

EG: So they were emptying the lager already. The lager wasn't that full. And we were taken out from the camp together, and we were very happy. And when we got to the bath, they made a second selection. Mengele came once more.

INT: To the-- where? When you got to the-- oh, the bath.

EG: The bath.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Mengele came for the second time. And he figured that [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the number of the people picked are higher than the number they were asked for. So he cut off about 200 people, and that's when they took my mother.

INT: Mhm. How did they do a selection?

EG: You go in line.

INT: And--

EG: And he says--

INT: --are you dressed?

EG: No. In this selection, I think, or the last one, we were dressed. But sometimes you were-- in the beginning, in the bath, when we went after the arrival, when Mengele picked, we were undressed.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But in this, we are dressed.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And just like this.

INT: Mhm.

EG: No word is being spoken, no-- only a gesture.

INT: Mhm. Did you know where your mother was going to go?

EG: When they took her away? Definitely.

INT: Mhm. Mhm. And where did you end up?

EG: We ended up all night waiting outside till the trains came. And then they took us and put up in the train.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we are taken into Germany.

INT: Mhm. Did you know-- did you have other clothing or food, when you got on the--

EG: They gave us an extra ration of bread. And they gave us a coat, some kind of a coat.

INT: Mhm. And had you any idea where you were going?

EG: No.

INT: Mhm. Who went with you?

EG: People from-- a couple people from my hometown, for different-- you know, we got all mixed up, because they took another group to another satellite camp what they had. And we arrived there. And we are taken-- that was a new camp.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There was not an existing camp where we were taken.

INT: Uh-huh. Did it have a name?

EG: Hainichen in Sachsen.

INT: Mhm. But that was the town.

EG: The town. The camp.

INT: And the camp had no name.

EG: No name.

INT: Was it a satellite to any of the camps?

EG: To Flossenburg.

INT: Uh-huh.

EG: That was the camp.

INT: Uh-huh. And what was there? Was there any structure, any--

EG: It was a regular building--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --two stories high, like a big loft building. And we had a huge room, with double bunk beds, with straw inside.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we got there, 250 Hungarian woman. And a couple weeks later, on the other floor we got the large transport from Lódz-- Littmanstadt.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I think about 200, 250 Polish women came.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And the only separation was in the front was a small room. And there was a, a German doctor from Vienna. And she got that room.

INT: Mhm. This-- how many were you total there?

EG: Approximately 500.

INT: Mhm. OK, we're going to change our tape again.

EG: Yeah, I know.

INT: Yeah.INT: Tape number four, interview with Eva Green-- Mrs. Eva Green. What were the sleeping arrangements in Hainichen?

EG: In Hainichen?

INT: Yeah.

EG: You had your individual bunk bed, made of wood, and a straw bag.

INT: Mhm. Were conditions better in Hainichen than in Auschwitz?

EG: Yes and no. Yes, because there was no danger of being killed. Nobody was killed. The work was brutal-- 12 hours a day, seven days a week.

And I don't know who, but they figured out, 12 hours you had in day shift, and then 12 hours in the night shift. So all together, you had only 24 hours. You shouldn't have Saturday or Sunday or something like that.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And 12 hours-- very little food, very little food. And otherwise, when you were working during the night and during the day, there were always a couple people, for whatever reason, they didn't go to work. They didn't have to go to work. So they spoke. So you couldn't really rest. It was very difficult.

And we had a oberscharfuhrerin who was the head, who was a real mean woman-- a real mean [INAUDIBLE]. And she beat people-- many-- you know, for whatever reason. And they said that she beat them until blood came out, always. That made her stop.

And there are two people who, who died of the consequences. One was a Hungarian woman, and one was a Polish woman from Litzmannstadt whom they said that she was one of the richest ladies there. And she never could accommodate herself.

She became sick. She never went to work. And then they beat her, and maybe she died for this reason or other reason.

A very good friend of mine died, who was laying next to me, because I think her kidney gave up. People gave up.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You know, people just--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --gave up. And during Passover or Easter, whatever, they gave us this pork cheese or something. And I ate a piece. And I don't know, from that, maybe not, I get jaundice. And I was yellow.

So I went to the doctor. And she looked at me. She looked at my eyes, and she said, you have jaundice. And that was it. There was no medicine. There was nothing.

Then from the fact that we didn't have vitamins, we had big sores, like the person-- then they went to the-- there was a, a revier was called. It wasn't a hospital. It was like a clinic, so to say. And people were there, with the pus running from them.

There was no food. And they had two medicines. They had a black ointment and a yellow ointment. And [LAUGHS] it depends-- they gave it to you. It was not doing anything. I still have, like, in my pinkies, you can see, and here in my leg--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --where I had the sores.

INT: Mhm. What kind of work were you doing?

EG: I was working in an ammunition factory where they made cannons. And I was sawing, like cutting with an electric saw, on a machine. They gave you a sizable piece of metal, and you had to cut it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They set the machine to the size--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and you had to cut it. And they brought barrels. And day and night, and day and night, for 12 hours. And it's a very tiring thing.

As a matter of fact, one day I was already toward the end. I was in a daze, because we didn't have the food. And I came, and we are standing in line to get our ration. And the oberscharfuhrerin came to me, and she gave me a big punch. That's the only one I got-- on my face.

And she says, do you know-- in German-- what means sabotage? I was figuring, I know what means sabotage. I'm not going to open my mouth here.

INT: Hm.

EG: So it turned out-- maybe my fault, maybe not my fault-- the, the saw cracked-- on which one I was working. I never realized it right away. I didn't know. Maybe cracked, maybe didn't crack. That's what she was saying. And she felt that it's sabotage.

We had a couple old Germans, there. And when we arrived there I heard-- overheard that they were saying that so young, and already like criminals or-- because they didn't know in the beginning.

And then, when we had a couple Frenchmen working there. And there was one who knew that I spoke French. And I spoke to him a little bit.

But it was very dangerous. And they didn't know. Like, he came on Monday, and he told me that they went somewhere on the camp and they found some green grass, whatever it was. And they made a soup or something from it, some food. How you tell him [LAUGHS] that we cannot go out. We don't go to the camp. We don't go nowhere.

And I remember-- when it was-- I think, April the 10th-- Roosevelt died-- or the 9th, or one of the days, I don't know, today. And he came to told me that President Roosevelt, il est mort. And I felt so sad. I was so upset. I figured, now it's the end of the world is coming.

And then I had a very good friend, a very intelligent girl. Her father was a very famous lawyer in Budapest. And she kind of gave, gave up. And she, she didn't she didn't wanted to eat [INAUDIBLE] She didn't eat.

As a matter of fact, the sign when you are already almost to go-- they had, like, a tin cup, and they filled it half with sugar, and they gave it to the person. That was the goodbye present.

And she died there. And they let me go to the cemetery when she was buried. They rolled her up in brown paper. And we went. And it's in the Hainichen cemetery, at the edge of the cemetery.

INT: They actually had funerals, in--

EG: It was-- yeah. They dug the grave, and they put her there.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I mean, there was no person--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --rabbi or any religious official.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But they let me go-- I think, me and another friend.

INT: You asked permission, or they said, anybody-- that you could go, or--

EG: They said it. You don't ask there.

INT: Ah. What happened to you when she slapped you and, and--

EG: Nothing.

INT: --about sabotage? Were you accused of anything?

EG: She felt, or they felt, that I cracked the--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --[INAUDIBLE]-- the machine, the saw.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I don't know if it cracked or not. I--

INT: Were you punished, other than just being slapped?

EG: No. No, no, no, she just gave me a big slap. I never got a slap in my life.

INT: Yeah. Were you ever punished in, in the camp?

EG: No.

INT: No.

EG: No. I tell you, I was a nonentity-- nonentity. I never had job, I never had honors. I never had punishment. I always did what they said, and I kept my mouth shut.

INT: Mhm.

EG: That was the way to survive. And something else, also--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --ever day, I went down, and I washed myself in ice-cold water.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I was coughing like a maniac, but I felt that I have to do it for myself.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I washed, I got myself [INAUDIBLE], and I washed. The had a little soap. They had a little rag, you know?

INT: What kind of clothing did you wear?

EG: I had this dress that they gave me at Auschwitz. That was a big blue dress from somebody. And I saved a couple rations from my bread. And there was a woman seamstress and made it shorter and tighter. And it looked more normal.

INT: And you only had the one dress.

EG: That was [INAUDIBLE].

INT: How did you keep it clean?

EG: That was it. Clean and dirty. It was, like, navy-blue-- I don't know-- kind of a wool.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: And I had a coat-- a coat. It was, like, grey and black, you know? In a V.

INT: A tweed.

EG: [GERMAN], what they call it.

INT: Yeah.

EG: And on the coat, and on the dress, on the back, they paint a long stripe, with white paint. So if by any reason you'd run away-- and when your hair grew a little bit, they cut off a stripe. Because, you know, we were started completely bald.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And then, when-- after, let's say, six months or eight months, you have a little hair, and then they cut in the center.

INT: Mhm. OK. You said you were-- you were never sick there, other than from the pus and--

EG: And I was coughing, and I had jaundice.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But bad sick, no. I, I mean, in normal circumstances, maybe it I would have been bad sick. But there--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --already we knew that, if you go to bed-- even though from there already did they did not.

INT: Mhm. Were you-- you were aware that Roosevelt had died. Were you--

EG: Yeah.

INT: --aware of the conditions that the war was in?

EG: No. I heard, a year later, that they landed in Normandy-- took me a year. Because when I came back, they never mentioned it. And somewhere in Bucharest, I read it in a newspaper-- was at the university-- something about the landing. I didn't know it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We knew that the war is going to end, because when we were transported, already was very near, the--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the front was very near.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Satu Mare, early October, it was already occupied.

INT: Mhm. How long did you stay in Hainichen?

EG: Hainichen, till April.

INT: So you were there from October to April.

EG: April.

INT: And what-- where did you go after that?

EG: After that, they put us in these death marches.

INT: How did they do that?

EG: One morning, they said, everybody line up. And we went on the first transport.

INT: And this was April?

EG: April, yeah.

INT: Where did you go?

EG: We went to a neighboring town that was, they said, about 40 kilometers. And there they put us in a train. And they went a little bit with the train. And then, then we stopped in a place.

We were going to the Czech frontier. Because, you know, Saxony is almost-- is bordering with Czechoslovakia--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --with Czechia, today-- the Czech Republic, to be very accurate. And we stayed there. And we didn't have any food, no food at all, no food at all. Somebody from the kitchen said that there were 5 kilo of margarine, margarine. And the commandant, the lady who was in charge, you know, the oberscharfuhrerin, stole it. So we didn't have no food.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They didn't give us no food, nothing, nothing at all. When we had an open wagon, and we are very happy when there were bombardments. There were bombardments.

INT: Who was-- do you know who--

EG: The Americans.

INT: Oh, mhm.

EG: But-- and once, I, I really had the-- that's the thing that I enjoyed, that on the other trucks, was coming a big train. And the door was open. And fancy furniture and the fancy German lady, sitting on top of the furniture. They were German, probably high officials. And this area was becoming Russian, so they were going toward--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the American.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And, to tell you also, in Hainichen I was so skinny that-- I have very big hip bones. And my hip bones got to the skin. And I had two red, like, circles.

INT: Hmm.

EG: Because the hip bones--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --you know--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --the come, like, in a V. And you just have skin on top of them. And they were hurting.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So I couldn't sleep on the side. Because--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --food was very, very little--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --very miserable food.

INT: Yeah. How long were you marching on the death march?

EG: Seven days it took.

INT: Uh-huh.

EG: Then we started marching again--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --till we arrived to Theresienstadt.

INT: Mhm. Did you go through any communities, any cities?

EG: Sides, yes.

INT: Mhm.

EG: As a matter of fact, once, when we stopped, two girls decided that they're going leave. And there was a bridge, and they crossed the bridge. And they, they're going to go in the village. And this was really at the end of the war. And the kind villagers brought them back.

INT: Mhm. Did the villagers ever talk to you or say anything?

EG: No.

INT: No.

EG: No, no.

INT: By the way, were you marching on the death march, only women or men and women?

EG: Only women. And we were told that anybody who can't march is going to be shot.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And there were a couple people who couldn't march, and they got a horse and buggy, and that was bringing them. But the truth is that they deposited them in Theresienstadt.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They didn't kill them.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They said they're going to kill them, that everybody--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --should march. And if you can't march, you're going to be killed. But they didn't do it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But when we got to Theresienstadt, the Germans disappeared.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But they came--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --that was the end of April.

INT: On these marches, did any villagers offer food?

EG: No--

INT: OK.

EG: --nobody never offered nothing.

INT: Yeah. Where'd you sleep?

EG: We slept, when we were in the wagon. They had an open wagon on the train.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: Only, the train didn't go.

INT: I see.

EG: They, they would put us there.

INT: Uh-huh. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] When you came to Theresienstadt, you say the Germans were all gone.

EG: Yes. I, I mean, I didn't see any German. Maybe--

INT: So what did you see, in Theresienstadt?

EG: No authority, only Jewish people.

INT: And what did you know at that time of what was happening with the war? Was the--

EG: Nothing.

INT: --war over?

EG: No. No.

INT: No.

EG: The war was over, maybe 10 more days or 12 more days--

INT: Uh-huh.

EG: --or something.

INT: So what'd you do in Theresienstadt?

EG: We stayed. We had a room and a bed-- many in a room. And we got food. And somebody-- not much food, but was good food, in the sense that what they gave us was barley, a lot of cooked barley, and some other cereal.

INT: Mhm.

EG: That's all they gave us. Because very many people-- I mean, everybody got diarrhea, but typhoid was rampant.

INT: Yeah. So who was running Theresienstadt, if the Germans weren't there and the Allies had not yet come in?

EG: The Jews.

INT: The Jews were-- which Jews?

EG: I think. I don't know. I never went to the center. [INAUDIBLE] we had in this kaserne, and not far was the center. We passed by, but I don't know.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Maybe there were still from the world hierarchy, I don't know.

INT: Uh-huh.

EG: You know--

INT: The kaserne is the barracks.

EG: The barracks, yeah.

INT: Mhm. Which barracks were you in?

EG: Dresden.

INT: Uh-huh. And were you-- how many were you in the barracks?

EG: I don't know--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --how many, but we were-- there were men, also. In the other room, there was a man who was a doctor, and he got typhoid fever. He died, even.

INT: Mhm.

EG: He was also from Transylvania.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There were rooms where they had men.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we spoke to each other, all day.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We didn't have to do nothing. And we-- nobody told us nothing. We didn't have to do-- we were--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --realizing that the end of the war is--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --here.

INT: Did the Germans accompany you to Theresienstadt?

EG: Yes.

INT: And then? They--

EG: They disappeared.

INT: They just disappeared.

EG: When we walked into Theresienstadt, we didn't see them anymore.

INT: And what date was that, do you know?

EG: I think it was April 27, but I wouldn't bet my life on it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But I think was almost positive. I can be a day off.

INT: Mhm. Mhm. What was the general mood in Theresienstadt? I mean--

EG: People were-- everybody's mind was the big question, who is alive?

INT: Mhm.

EG: If--

INT: The war was not over yet.

EG: No, but we knew already, you know, that we are toward the end of the war.

INT: When did you actually know that the war was over?

EG: The day when the war ended, and we looked out, there was, like, a road. And all kinds of Germans were going there, soldiers and not soldiers, and people were going. And they were-- took away their rucksacks and everything and were throwing them away. Not because we need it but because, just, you know, the anger.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And then they brought in some Germans to clean the place and to clean the latrines and-- from the neighboring--

INT: Yeah. Who--

EG: --town.

INT: --who's they? Who--

EG: I would say, the management.

INT: Yeah.

EG: So I don't know the--

INT: So in other words, you did not know who was running Theresienstadt--

EG: No, I didn't.

INT: --at the time.

EG: No, no, no.

INT: What language what they-- were they speaking, the people who were running--

EG: I don't know.

INT: No.

EG: Maybe. German.

INT: No. Uh-huh.

EG: Maybe Hungarian. I don't know.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Maybe Russian. There was a Russian commandant. That we knew. But they said that, because of the behavior of the Russian soldier-- he was a Jewish man-- I mean, the top-secret in [INAUDIBLE]-- and he didn't wanted the soldiers to come in there.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And he was absolutely right.

INT: So who did liberate you? What army?

EG: Must have been the Russian Army, but I haven't seen a Russian soldier.

INT: Uh-huh. You never saw Russian soldiers there.

EG: No, no.

INT: What soldiers did you see?

EG: Nothing. I saw the Germans going away.

INT: Oh. When did you know the war was over? How did you know?

EG: When they said-- everybody knew it. And then they brought-- the Germans were running on the side--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --on the road, you know, not far away. You-- and the door was open-- I mean, the gate. And you could go out, and you looked.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And you saw Germans, going. And they got dressed up in civilian and some in uniform. And they were all running. And they were not the glorious German Army. If a Jew who weighed 90 pounds told him, give me your rucksack, give me this, they were giving away everything.

INT: Mhm. What did you do after--

EG: We stayed--

INT: --after that?

EG: We stayed--

INT: How long did you stay there?

EG: I stayed till June, I think, end of June.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because they said that they have transports going, you know, to Romania. Because--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --there was hardly any communication or anything. And already, food was more plentiful. Not rich food, but there was what to eat--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in Theresienstadt, a lot of barley and kasha and, you know, this type of stuff.

INT: Mhm. And how did you leave?

EG: I have the paper. We went, and we signed up that we are from this-and-this town. And they told us what day a transport is going to go. So the transport left in June.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And it took us to Budapest.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And in Budapest-- you know, these trains were not going-- one, two, three hours-- stopped for six hours, and then one, two, three hours.

And we were attacked, by the way. We had an arrangement, that the women were inside and outside-- you know how the trains are. They have, like, a--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --small front. The men were there. And a drunk Russian soldier came. And he came, and he started, you know, with a woman, with a religious woman, and she started screaming. And I was sitting there, and I told her that-- don't say nothing.

And the soldier then came and took out a gun and put it at my head. [LAUGHS] And I read once before that, if somebody's in this stage, talk to them. So I started talking to him. And we said that we are Romanian. Because we knew, by that time, you know, that Hungary was on the wrong side and the Romanians got out from the war almost a year earlier.

So he was talking about Stalingrad-- you know, everybody was from Stalingrad and [? hero ?] and [? the ?] Russian-- I don't know what. And then, I tried to tell him-- concentration camp, concentration camp. And he said I should show the number. So I said, no number.

So he started screaming-- Germans [NON-ENGLISH]. That's right, I mean-- [LAUGHS] and--

INT: What does that mean?

EG: That you are the whore of a German captain.

INT: Oh, OK.

EG: And everybody was quiet. And I was telling him, you know-- in Romanian I spoke to him. I made myself-- you know? He didn't understand anything, but he was--

And all of a sudden, God helped us, because they were whistling. And, you know, we were in one train here, and they were in the other train-- that the train is going to leave. Because he was telling-- Stalingrad and hero and I don't know-- that his mother, father, you know, they-- everybody was in from Stalingrad, and everybody was a big hero. Many were, I would imagine, but they were very-- lowlifes--

INT: Mm.

EG: --some of them, you know? And so the train was whistling and started going. And then he run out, and he--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --went to his train.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And then went to Budapest. And we stayed, I think, a day in Budapest, till it started going, the train. And then I got to Satmar.

INT: How did you connect with your family?

EG: I wrote from Theresienstadt-- somebody went to Prague and decided that he-- met an American soldier. He speaks English, and he's going to mail the letter.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But that letter never came, never arrived. When I was in Bucharest, I was waiting and waiting. And I wrote letters, but no answer.

INT: Hm.

EG: And I sent a telegram.

INT: You were in Bucharest, before you went to Budapest?

EG: No, no no, after-- Bucharest, when I went in August.

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: I sent a telegram. By that time, you could send a telegram.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I got a telegram answer.

INT: Who did you send a telegram to?

EG: My father.

INT: Oh. I see.

EG: I had the address. And then after, I got a telegram also, that we want you to come to-- with us, your father's brother.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because my father wasn't a citizen still. And that time, you know, was no entrance for DPs or something. And my uncle was a very powerful man in Mexico.

INT: Mhm.

EG: He had a lot of connections and a lot of money. And he wasn't stingy with the bribery [LAUGHS] also. So he wrote that we should go.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But it took us two years till we get the passport, because we didn't leave--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the country illegally. Everybody left illegally, but we got the passport.

INT: OK. Let me go back a little bit. You went from Theresienstadt to Budapest.

EG: Yeah, but I stayed only a day.

INT: Who helped you get there?

EG: The train.

INT: Was there any agency?

EG: No.

INT: No?

EG: I didn't go to agencies.

INT: Yeah. Did you-- did you go to a DP camp?

EG: No.

INT: No, why?

EG: I don't know.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I never went there.

INT: So--

EG: First, I wanted to go home.

INT: Yeah?

EG: I need to see if some-- if they-- you know, a, a thread of hope. I mean--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --maybe somebody's there. But nobody was there. And there was a-- in the Jewish hospital, you could go-- you could stay-- they gave you food.

But in the meantime, I had a friend, and she had an apartment. She had her uncle's house. Her uncle came back. And they already were doing business, by that time and they--

INT: Where was this? In--

EG: In Satu Mare.

INT: Oh. So you went-- you did get to Satu Mare.

EG: Yeah. And in Satu Mare. And I stayed in her house. And there was plenty food. There was even a maid and everything, because that time, the business people, you know, who came back were doing big businesses.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because there was nothing, before. So whatever they could-- they had their old connection with their factory, with this and that. You could get, I don't know, two rolls of linen or five rolls of cloth.

INT: Mhm. How long did you stay in--

EG: I stayed there till about September.

INT: And then?

EG: Then I went to Bucharest.

INT: Uh-huh.

EG: Because I knew I had an aunt in Bucharest, and I had an uncle in Bucharest.

INT: Oh.

EG: And I wanted to go to school.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So I went there.

INT: Where did you get money?

EG: You didn't need money.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The trains were going-- like, I went to Bucharest? The train-- I went on the train. It was--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --a train.

INT: Mhm. How long did you stay in Budapest, by the way?

EG: No, only a day--

INT: Oh, only a day, uh-huh.

EG: --two, two days, till the train--

INT: When you came back to Satu Mar-- Satu Mare, who was in your house? Did you go back?

EG: Not to my house, no.

INT: Oh. Did you go to your friend whom you left the--

EG: Yeah, and he--

INT: --jewelry with?

EG: --gave me some clothes, what he had-- she had-- some clothes and some jewelry. Yes.

INT: What was her reaction when she saw you?

EG: She was a very plain woman, you know? I was teaching her to read and write. She was so plain.

And she said that everybody was telling the same story. They were taken to America, and they were taken to Palestine. And nobody was killed. I mean, you know, that-- even today, they tell you that story, if you go there--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --you know, the story goes that--

INT: Mhm. Yeah. What did you study in Bucharest?

EG: Languages, mainly. Letter of philosophy is the department.

INT: Mhm. And what were your hopes? What were you planning to do?

EG: I don't know. Maybe something, because I spoke so many languages-- maybe get the foreign service, something. If not, high-school teacher, you know--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --something.

INT: What was your life like in Bucharest?

EG: In Bucharest--

INT: You were a young woman.

EG: Yeah. In Bucharest I had social life already. I had friends-- male friends and female friends-- and we went here and there. It wasn't a very safe life, still, the first year after--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the war ended, you know, but we, we had some kind of a life.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We went to the movie. We met.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But I didn't take any help from organizations.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I got some money from some people who knew-- they gave me money, that they're going to get it in America, what they did--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --you know?

INT: How was your health, when you came out of the camp?

EG: Good.

INT: You said you were very thin.

EG: Terrible thin. But then I blew up. I gained--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --so much weight, I really blew up. And I--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --little by little, I lost the weight.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I went for a checkup in Bucharest, to a doctor. And it's OK. I mean--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --I didn't have anything. The only thing what I might have gotten-- I don't know-- I went a couple years ago for a checkup. And the doctor gave me a very thorough checkup. He was doing everything what's in the book, you know?

INT: Mhm.

EG: So he says, I want a tuberculosis test. Because, when-- he saw some-- a little spot on the X-ray. And he gave me such a class that I should put it on my head, and I should watch, next day, and if it's a little red [INAUDIBLE].

So I do it. And I come home at night. Well, I didn't have to wait till next day. I didn't have to wait for nothing. My arm, from here to here, got red and all inflamated. [LAUGHS] So probably I got--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --when I was coughing, I got from somebody. But--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --he said it was totally cauterized, and I don't have nothing. But that's my story with--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --the TB.

INT: Mhm. When did you decide to come to this country?

EG: I didn't wanted to stay there, never. And when I saw the communists after a couple months, I, I didn't join any communist organization.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I knew my father was here, and then my uncle was there, and he offered, you know, that I-- we should go. He's going to arrange everything. And--

INT: Mm.

EG: --we're going to start a new life.

INT: OK. OK. Let me change the tape again.

EG: Oh my gosh.INT: Tape number five, interview with Mrs. Eva Green. How did you get out of Bucharest? How did you leave Bucharest?

EG: I applied for a passport. And that time, already, the king was leaving when I applied for the passport. That was in '47-- fall of '47. And I was kind of afraid that I'm not going to get a passport. But I was sick and tired from going to borders illegally, going, and going to DP camp, it had no appeal for me.

And I applied for passport. And that time, was very, very tough in Romania. People were really taken prisoners and very, very tough. The jails were full of people.

So I applied, and I waited and waited for months. All of a sudden, my aunt had a nice apartment in Bucharest, but a Romanian wanted her apartment, in the '40s, in Hitler's time, so she had to move out. But in Bucharest, the Jews were not deported or anything. She gave up her apartment.

And in a commercial building, on the fourth floor, she got a plenty bad apartment. You had to walk up-- it had, I think, three little rooms and a little kitchen, but walk up. And very poor facilities.

So there was a big shortage of apartments. And a man from the security came up. And she came to look for the apartment. Because she thought, if I'm going to leave, they're going to get the apartment.

So I had a very small room. And I took in the room. I said, this is your-- my room. Is this the room? So he wasn't very happy, because it wasn't an appealing room.

And then he asked me questions, what I'm doing. I am going to university. What I'm studying? And he said, why you want to leave the country? You have a future.

And I didn't say that I go to the United States, because by that time, my uncle bought me a visa to Nicaragua. Because to Mexico was very hard to get.

So I had this Nicaraguan visa. And I said, I am going to Nicaragua. That was, like, not something communist, not something capitalist. He didn't knew much about it.

And he asked me, but why you want to go? And I looked at him, and I said, look, if you were 22 years old and you had a chance to see the world, would you go to see it? He said yes. I said, [INAUDIBLE].

And he gave a favorable [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] opinion. And finally they gave me the passport. And then we had to go for the visa [NON-ENGLISH], it's called-- the exit visa. This was another story that drove you nuts. Because you never knew, they give it, they don't give it. They put this guy in jail. They put that guy in jail.

So finally I got it. And immediately, I left Bucharest. And I went to Prague. And my uncle, my aunt, and the two children came.

In Prague, HIAS was putting up, in a hotel. And they-- but my uncle paid for it, because they said right away that-- HIAS, from there, you know, announced-- advised them that it's a man of money. And he was willing to pay, so.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We stayed there for six weeks. In the beginning was very nice. I went sightseeing, and Prague, you know, beautiful city. And Masaryk jumped out of the window, was pushed out of the window, and the whole situation changed terrible. And I was very, very nervous, and were all very nervous, because we needed a French transit visa.

And finally, because we had the Nicaraguan papers-- what was a big fakery, it turned out later-- we got the French transit visa. So then we arrived in Paris. And in Paris I had-- my father had a brother in Paris.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Was married and had already a child. And I stayed with them [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] till my uncle wrote that he has a contact in Mexico that is [INAUDIBLE] immigration even today, it's very hard. And the president was, that time, Miguel Alemán. My uncle didn't have a contact. But the elected president that was coming in next year, he had somebody whom the president promised that we are going to get the papers. And that's how it happened that we are--

INT: What did you do, in France?

EG: In France, I took courses.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And we had a lot of friends, and we had a very good life.

INT: Mhm.

EG: We had even money. He sent me $1,000, as I remember. And he wrote that we should get-- you know, he figured probably that we're going [INAUDIBLE] or something, that we should buy nice clothes, he wanted, and everything, because he wanted to show off, I guess, between his friends that--

INT: Mhm.

EG: So-- and we went to theatres, to cinema. It was very cheap.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The opera was, like, $1, I mean, the cheapest ticket, standing, and museums. And we had a lot of--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --lot of--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --very good friends.

INT: Did you have any contact with any of the people you were with in Auschwitz or in Hainichen or--

EG: Sure! There are here very many, in New York.

INT: No, but in-- when-- during this time, did people--

EG: Yes.

INT: --stick together--

EG: Yes.

INT: --keep in touch--

EG: Yes, people stick very much together.

INT: Mhm.

EG: In-- as a matter of fact, in Paris, I had a very good friend. And she got married there. She got to Australia. I on to visit her. She comes to visit me.

INT: Mhm. But that's now. But at that time, did you have relationships--

EG: The time we were there?

INT: Yeah.

EG: They stayed in a hotel. Because in France, they have these cheap hotel. And they all stayed there-- all people from my town. And we met every afternoon, and we talked a lot--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and we went to--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --different places. Yes, it was--

INT: Mhm. Mhm. So when did you come to this continent?

EG: To this-- I left in '49. In Paris already, I got the visa. And there was a very big problem [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] for transportation. You couldn't cross the Atlantic. They didn't have boats, and they didn't have planes.

And finally, HIAS got a boat that was a mixed cargo and passenger that was going from France to Tampico-- that's a port in Mexico-- 30 days-- first stop in Antwerp, and the second stop was in Louisiana, in New Orleans.

No, second stop was in Cuba. And third stop was in New Orleans, Louisiana, and fourth stop in Tampico. And it took 30 days.

And I had my own cabin. And they had diplomats and all kinds of people. And in Mexico that time, they wouldn't allow Jews. So in my papers, my uncle said I should say 'agnostic,' because agnostic is accepted. So that time, what I had a Romanian passport, we didn't have religion. And the visa said agnostic.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: Because-- then, I-- we spent two days in Antwerp. I went looking for my aunt, but the building what I had the address was bombed. Then I went to Cuba, and there we were sightseeing in Cuba. Already I spoke Spanish. It was before Castro.

And then I went to New Orleans. My father came to New Orleans, because the only place in America-- even though I had a visa from Mexico, they wouldn't let me off the boat.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So he came.

INT: Mm.

EG: We stayed two days in New Orleans. And he came to visit me in the morning, and he stayed till in the evening, for two days. As a matter of fact, he had to take-- change the hotel that was near, because the second day was Shabbat.

INT: Mhm. Tell about the Shabbat [INAUDIBLE] observance. Were you able to observe during this period of time?

EG: In Mexico?

INT: No, not in Mexico. In the camps, and in--

EG: In the camps, no.

INT: --and after the--

EG: No. No.

INT: --and after liberation--

EG: No, very little--

INT: --when you were going--

EG: --very little--

INT: --back and forth?

EG: --very little, very little.

INT: Mhm. And in France and--

EG: In France also, because my uncle wasn't religious.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: My daughter calls him the, the last hot red-blooded communist.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Poor guy died a couple years ago.

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: And he was here. And, you know, he didn't spoke about it, but-- he wouldn't admit, but he realized--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --you know, the truth, but he still didn't admit it.

INT: Yeah. In the camps-- you had come from an observant home.

EG: Yeah. So everybody else wasn't-- or the majority.

INT: Yeah. How did you deal with, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] with God? Did you lose your faith--

EG: There was very little--

INT: --in God?

EG: I tell you, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] there was very little [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] spiritual thing-- really, very little. Some woman, you know, they prayed because they had the prayer and they were--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --like, religious. But after a while, anything that's spiritual, the life is so cruel and--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --so barbaric, and you are so hungry and cold and frozen, that, I don't know. You know, I hear stories that they were this spiritual and that spiritual and turned religious before. We weren't-- I, I couldn't vouch for it, I tell you--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --because my experience is that the cruelty of the daily life took the spirituality out of the people.

INT: So what kept you going?

EG: I don't know. I don't know. You know something? People say, why? Because it just happened. That's my theory. It just happened, you didn't die, because you didn't die. It keeped you going, you know?

Everybody has an inner drive that I don't want to die, basically. And people who died, they gave up. They gave up, you know, the fighting, so to say.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But that I should have spiritual thoughts about thinking this or that-- what we spoke, mainly in Hainichen, when we had a little time-- recipes, recipes, and recipes--

INT: [LAUGHS]

EG: --food, food, food. I'm telling you.

INT: Yeah.

EG: Or stories--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --about things that we did, or things that we saw, the things we are familiar, you know, that comes out from this terrible environment.

INT: How long did you stay in Tampico?

EG: In Tampico? Well, my uncle came, and he bought the [INAUDIBLE] government. No, ay, yi, yi. Fancy-schmancy. We went on an airplane right away to Mexico City.

INT: And--

EG: One of those-- you know what a Hail Mary airplane is?

INT: No.

EG: You don't know the Hail Mary airplane? [LAUGHS] Two-seater.

INT: Oh, the Hail Maria plane. [INAUDIBLE].

EG: That were one of those.

INT: Yeah. So how long did you remain in Mexico?

EG: Five years, because I--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --couldn't leave before five years.

INT: What'd you do in Mexico?

EG: In Mexico, legally, I couldn't work. Because the law says you can't work for five years. But I worked. I supervised his business. I went and I made the accountings. You know, I counted, and I counted the money, and--

INT: Mhm.

EG: I, I helped in the business.

INT: Mhm.

EG: My uncle's wife wasn't Jewish.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And she had a very large family who lived with her, who were not Jewish, who were very ardent Catholics.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But she wasn't a bad person.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And she wanted-- I mean, she, she had the fancy relatives, and she wanted that his family should come. I guess she didn't have children, and she felt that he's was very good to her sister and her sister's children. And they left all the money to them, you know, all the buildings, all the money, everything.

INT: Mhm. When did you get to the United States?

EG: 1955.

INT: And where did you go?

EG: I came here to New York, and here was my father. I came with a visitor's visa.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I met all the people from my hometown. And they introduced me to my future husband.

INT: Mhm. Was he from your hometown?

EG: No, from another one, northern Transylvania--

INT: Mhm. Mhm. Yeah.

EG: --but not very--

INT: And was he a survivor also?

EG: Yes. But he wasn't in camp. He was in labor department.

INT: In a labor camp.

EG: And in October '44, he hid. You know, he left the Hungarian advance, backward, and he stayed in, in the barn of a, I think, Russian peasant--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --or something. And he has a brother here who is a survivor, who was in Auschwitz.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: As a matter of fact, we donated to the Holocaust Museum in Washington. And they said that this is the only one. They were very impressed.

My brother-in-law, when we were in Auschwitz, they gave to send people postcards, to fool the world, you know? And the name of Auschwitz, the code name in German, was 'Wannsee.'

INT: Mhm.

EG: I don't know if you've heard of it or no.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Maybe because of the Wannsee Conference. And--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --my brother-in-law knew that his brother is in the labor battalion. And they-- he wrote with a pencil. He sent him in the labor battalion. And [SPEAKING GERMAN]-- 'We are all here.' [SPEAKING GERMAN]-- because he figured, if he writes something, [? so-- ?] and he gave it. And it has the stamp of the German censor, and it has the stamp of the Hungarian censor. And initial, the German signed.

It says, on the top, Wannsee, not Auschwitz. The word Auschwitz--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --is nonexistent.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And they said that they never-- nobody gave it to them, [? one. ?] I brought it, and they took it with pincers, and they put it--

INT: Mhm. Yeah.

EG: --between papers. And they made such big fuss.

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: And I had-- I pulled it out from my pocketbook.

INT: Who were your friends here in New-- in New York?

EG: In New York? 50-50. I have very good friends, people from Europe. So we stick together. Some from my hometown. Some-- I have a very good friend I went to the University of Bucharest together, somebody else from Bucharest. And then I have American friends, here in the neighborhood.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You know, a woman from [INAUDIBLE]--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --that I met, and a woman from this building.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So I have friends. Not-- I don't have time-- you know, I don't belong to the luncheon crowd.

INT: Yeah. [LAUGHS].

EG: I might send them a check, but I don't show up-- only for the Hadassah luncheon.

INT: You have two children.

EG: Yeah.

INT: And how did your experiences in camp, in Auschwitz and in the other camp, impact on the way you raised them?

EG: Probably-- I am not a psychologist, but probably, indirectly. You know, they say that the mothers are maybe more pushy. They want to have them more succeed. Or maybe it's myself, that I am success-oriented. I don't need to work at the age of 75, believe me--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and I still work. And [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] we-- they knew it, because I always said-- I didn't make a big thing about it. But I remember, they were watching television once. And I think Stevie was five or something, and Carol was seven or a little bit less.

And they showed something on the news or something, German soldiers marching. And maybe some inmates were marching also, something, like a short-- a minute or two. And they're screaming, Mommy, Mommy! Come! You are on television.

INT: Mhm.

EG: [CRYING AND LAUGHING]

INT: [INAUDIBLE].

EG: You know--

INT: How much do they-- did they know or do they know about your experiences?

EG: They know that I was in Auschwitz. They know--

INT: Mhm. Mhm.

EG: --about my mother.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They know that I worked in a factory.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They know.

INT: Mhm. They asked, or you--

EG: A little bit asked. And Carol knows much more, because Carol took a lot of courses and books. And she is kind of a--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --mini expert in Holocaust. She wanted to make on that-- because she wrote the history thesis on Barnard. And--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --I have this woman who is a head of Yad Vashem call-- Dr. [INAUDIBLE] and the Yad Vashem publication. She said, don't you dare to let her. Don't you let her that involved in Holocaust. She never married. I mean-- and she's a very well-known-- if you open any book, you know, her name is there. But--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --that's what she has.

INT: Mhm. Did your experience influence the way you raised them Jewishly, their Jewish education?

EG: I don't know, because I always felt Jewish, you see? When the Germans came in, some people toyed with the idea that-- or under Hungarian-- that if they convert-- and I never had the idea.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I always felt, you are born Jewish, you die Jewish, finish.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There is no if--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --and no betweens.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And that's what I told them, all the time. Religious, not religious, doesn't make any difference. You are--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --Jewish, you are born Jewish, you are dying Jewish.

INT: Mhm. What kind of education did they have Jewishly?

EG: Oh! They went to Jewish school. I never sent them to public school.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They always went to yeshiva, to day school.

INT: Uh-huh. I see. Did they ever visit Satmar?

EG: No.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I went with my husband for one day. And-- because we went to his hometown, because his father died before the war, and we went to the grave.

INT: Mhm.

EG: He wanted to visit his father's grave. And I tell you, Satmar basically couldn't offer them much.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because it's a totally changed town.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There are no-- there's not a single Jew.

INT: Mhm.

EG: There is not a single person--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --that I know. I could go, show the school, but-- I wanted to take them, but no-- you know, Carol has a lot of children. And maybe, if I'm still alive--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --one day I take them.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But--

INT: Are they involved in Holocaust activities and--

EG: Carol, when she was here, she went to the second-generation meetings a couple time.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But now she has small children, she doesn't go. And Steven doesn't.

INT: Mhm. Do you dream very much about your experiences?

EG: I don't dream.

INT: Did you ever?

EG: I am not a dreamer. What I had-- I think it's worse than a dream. When I came back, for a long time, and everybody said, I used to scream-- but scream very, very strongly at night, for a couple of years.

INT: In your sleep.

EG: In my sleep.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Not every day. Maybe not every other day. But from time to time-- terrible. So, maybe I had some dreams that I lost before I woke up. But I screamed.

INT: Mhm. Mhm. What--

EG: You think--

INT: Mhm.

EG: You-- and the older you get, the more you think--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --not so much of your personal experience but maybe of your mother or of your family.

INT: Mhm. Mhm. What was, for you, the most difficult time?

EG: I don't know. I think what's very tragic, when I knew that my mother is being burned right now, or taken away from me.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I think that was maybe the worst.

INT: Mhm.

EG: Because I knew it. And I never fooled myself, you know, that-- I wasn't even 20, because I was liberated already when I was 20.

But I don't know. Maybe I was more pessimistic. When we are walking toward [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Theresienstadt, they had a very big chimney. I don't know what kind of thing was using.

And I was 100% sure that these chimneys from a crematorium, and they're going to kill us. And I was figuring, all these idiots [LAUGHS] around me, they don't know where they are going. I am the only one who knows where we are going.

INT: Mhm.

EG: I mean, you know, you have it inside of you. And whatever they did, whatever-- you never trusted it. You always thought-- the-- you know, this is a an Auschwitz experience, and you always think, the end is going to be in the smoke.

INT: Mhm. Have you ever gone back to Theresienstadt or Hainichen?

EG: I went back to Theresienstadt. And I went back to Auschwitz.

INT: Mhm.

EG: For one day in Auschwitz and one day in Theresienstadt. And nobody wanted to come with me. And then I met my relatives in Budapest.

INT: Mm.

EG: Because my husband has a brother who was killed [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] in the last days of war, at the Austro-Hungarian frontier.

INT: Mhm.

EG: He was in labor battalion. And he had, I think, typhoid or something. And at night, he left his tent and wanted to go to the bathroom or something. So a soldier shoot him

INT: Mhm.

EG: [INAUDIBLE]. And he is in a common grave.

INT: Mhm.

EG: But his name is there.

INT: Mhm.

EG: So my brother-in-law went already, but he said, you go to Budapest.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And I brought a lot of pictures about--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --his brother's grave.

INT: What was your experience in visiting Auschwitz and Theresienstadt?

EG: Auschwitz-- I tell you, they sanitized it.

INT: Mhm.

EG: It's not Auschwitz. It's full-- it was full of grass. I mean, when I was there, there was no grass. The barracks are taken all down, you know?

I wanted to go to the archives. They said that you can't go in. So, you know, it's like a big cover-up story, in my opinion, still.

INT: Mhm.

EG: They-- the guide whom I had-- I took a guide-- was very good.

INT: Mhm.

EG: And she was a very decent person. She looked like a academician in her 30s--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --maybe 40-- not fancy lady. And she knew the stuff. And she had a lot of-- but I didn't tell her that I was there. I, I didn't have the-- how should I tell you-- the rapport to tell that woman, that Polish woman, that I was here.

INT: Mhm.

EG: The only person whom I told was some very rich Australian old couple, with their daughter and grandchildren--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --Jewish people, who--

INT: Yeah. How about in Theresienstadt? How-- what was your--

EG: In Theresienstadt, there it was much different atmosphere. I don't know if I am right or if it's in the back of my mind and I'm speaking subjectively. They let me in the archive. I have here the pages, with what I arrived from Hainichen. I had the page when I left Theresienstadt.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the people were not that-- you know-- I didn't feel animosity--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --in between the people. In Poland, the people are very religious. Every church is full during the day, whenever you look. Every window has the pope's picture.

In Prague, only 20% of the population is registered under any kind of religion.

INT: Hm.

EG: And I visited the Jewish sites, you know? I visited them when I was--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --first time--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --in Prague. But Prague is very different.

INT: Mhm.

EG: You feel freer. I don't know, in Poland, I felt like something is--

INT: Mm.

EG: --pressing me, you know? Like I didn't go to eat I. Ate only one piece of herring. And I said, I am not going to give them my money.

And they give very big breakfast. I picked up two rolls, and I put some butter on the rolls, and I that's what I took with me. I, I wouldn't have bought a needle if they would have given me $1,000.

INT: Mm.

EG: And I should give them $5 [LAUGHS] worth for a needle. I mean, you have it in you. And whatever I saw from the Polish part wasn't anything better than the German part.

INT: Why do you think you survived?

EG: I don't know. You know the word bashert?

INT: Yeah. Mhm.

EG: Maybe. I think that's it. I, I, I-- [LAUGHS] there is no merit of mine, absolutely no merit of mine. It happened.

INT: Why are you choosing to tell your story, again, now-- because I know you told your story once.

EG: I don't know--

INT: Why are you--

EG: --because--

INT: --doing it again?

EG: --there are so many deniers, that somebody has to prove it, that-- and if the more people-- they're going to have 10,000 tapes or 20,000 tapes, then 100 years from now, they cannot start again. Somebody like they had a trial in London that it didn't happen. Because it's a very big deniers' story, all over the world. Even in Hungary.

Today, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they go to the villages, and they going to say, oh, they were taken to Palestine, or they were taken to America--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --the peasants. What they know very well--

INT: Yeah.

EG: --that the Jews were killed in Auschwitz. It's a nonacceptance, or they don't want to accept, or the government-- as a matter of fact, I read in the paper that in Budapest, they wanted to make some kind of-- I don't even say Holocaust museum, but some kind of a museum in reference to the Hungarian Jews and whatever. And the preopening, they invited the heads of the Jewish community.

And they looked at the pictures. And all the pictures were showing that the Germans were doing, and the Germans were putting the Jews, and the Germans were doing this, and the Germans were doing that. The Germans, they were maybe dictating. But the Hungarians were following. Because in a city like Satmar, where they took 12,000 Jews, you couldn't find 10 Germans.

So they did-- so it said that this is not in accordance with the reality. And they wouldn't go to the opening or they-- so supposedly, they--

INT: Mhm.

EG: --don't know-- I don't know if they're going to change it or whatever, but there was no opening.

INT: Is there anything you want to add to your story? Because we're going to be showing your pictures in a few minutes.

EG: No, that-- only thing what I tell you, for all the bad stuff-- I came to America, and America has been very good to us. I can say nothing, you know-- because we made a life. We succeeded very well economically. We succeeded socially.

I have two children, who, thanks God, are successful on their own. And I have 10 grandchildren. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] it's a proof that we're going to live. It's not so easy to get rid of us.

